            <div xml:id="d140">

                <pb facs="0191" n="181" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–2345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-23T12:00:00-04:00">January 23,
                            1945—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>117. The President has let me read your exceedingly interesting telegram of
                    January 14, No. 141500 NCR 6810,<anchor n="40" xml:id="d140-f1"/> and I want you
                    to know how much we appreciate the constructive work that you are doing.
                    Appropriate steps have been taken to explore the difficulties you have had with
                    our own military subordinates.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
